[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) was merged on subject lines ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)).
Nobody had to read the bodies, so nobody did. We read all of them. The ticket holds three problems.
Each has its own ticket in [the split repo](https://github.com/pcierpiatka/s01e03-tenant-t3), with the count below.

<Callout kind="warn">
Three problems, not one. The fix queued for #4471 covered only the first; the desk closed the
second as won't fix (see the update below).
</Callout>

## What the bodies show

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} />

### `parser` · the shared parser reads day-first dates month first

Every tenant on file writes the day first. The shared parser reads every date month first,
so 7 April becomes 4 July. This is the bug #4471 was opened for. It covers every channel and
every tenant, and intake tags most of it right.

### `hint` · booking-v3's form says MM/DD/YYYY and reads DD/MM

Three tenants run booking-v3. Its date field tells people to type month first, then the
form reads what they typed the tenant's way. The symptom is the same swap, but the fix is in
the form, not the parser: the kb's own `causeOf` already keeps it apart, and the old page
rendered it as "no ticket of its own". Now it has one. It only reaches us through the
`form` channel at those three tenants.

### `one-day` · bookings shift by one day, no month flip

A small group of bodies show no swap at all: "Invite says Tuesday 12 May, calendar says
Monday 11 May", "Tuesday 6 October showed as Monday", a client in Lisbon and a client in
Toronto holding the same meeting on different days, every June booking one day off. The
desk's notes already read them as a one-day move but filed them under `parser` by tenant.
They are not a date-order bug. They look like a timezone or day-boundary bug, and a parser
fix will not touch them. The desk's note is the list we use, including R-41236 ("the invite
arrived before I made the booking"), whose body shows no date. They stay filed under
`parser` by tenant and are counted there, as the kb's own table does; this ticket covers
them as their own problem so the fix is not lost in the footnote.

## How we counted

- One id, one report: the export has duplicate rows, and `uniqueById` keeps one per id.
- A blind reader per tenant classified every body by what it says, seeing no tag and no note.
- Where the body and the kb's rule disagreed, an adjudicator placed the report and a second
  reader re-read it with a seeded sample of the rest.
- Bodies with no dates at all were placed by the kb's rule for their tenant and channel.
- Counts follow the desk's own view, as exported: `parser` and `hint` split the 412 the way
  `causeOf` does, and the one-day reports are filed under `parser` and footnoted (`readAs`),
  so they are counted there and in their own ticket.
- The table above is computed from the id lists at render time. The same lists feed
  `tools/counts.py`, which checks that `parser` and `hint` place every report exactly once and
  that every one-day report is filed under `parser`.

## Desk update · #4471 closed 05 October

<Callout>
<strong>parser</strong> · fixed · <code>fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales</code><br />
<strong>hint</strong> · won't fix · booking-v3 form retired
</Callout>

Both tickets are closed in the split repo with these outcomes. The one-day reports close with
the parser ticket they are filed under; their bodies still show a one-day shift, so if it comes
back after the parser fix it needs a new ticket outside #4471.

## What to change

- Dedupe on cause, not subject, during incidents about dates: `sameCause` already exists.
- Keep the one-day bodies in view: a parser fix does not explain a client in Lisbon and a
  client in Toronto holding the same meeting on different days.

